{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices (often by ~5–15 EUR), especially from ~day 5 onward. Apply a downward level shift to the whole path, increasing with horizon.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: when the market transitions into a drawdown, the model continues to project sideways-to-up prices. Add a regime check: if recent returns are negative and volatility rising, tilt the forecast slope downward (stronger for 2–4 week horizon).",
    "Overshoot after local highs: after a run-up (recent 10–20d strong), the model extrapolates strength and misses subsequent mean reversion. Damp forecasts when history shows an extended rally (e.g., price above short MA by >1–2σ).",
    "Underestimates drawdown magnitude: model captures direction only weakly; realized declines are steeper and faster. Increase downside beta: scale negative forecast changes by >1 (or cap upside changes) during selloff regimes.",
    "Horizon-specific error growth: forecast error increases with lead time (small at days 1–3, large at days 10–30). Use a horizon-dependent correction: minimal adjustment near-term, progressively larger downward adjustment and stronger mean-reversion pull for longer horizons."
  ]
}
